//! Batch 341 -- the `metal-graph` `kernel_opt` distill (Plan 350 /
//! Research 383): the walk-24-adjacent user-directed verdict, the corpus's
//! FIRST dedicated graph-exec-LIFECYCLE source (the Metal patch/eligibility
//! rules transcribe to the CUDA-graph vocabulary directly).
//!
//! Source: `robbiemu/metal-graph` @
//! `0cd855b72d23c44aa0f7511aa6f01f694c00da39` (HEAD 2026-05-10, Apache-2.0,
//! `C11` + `ObjC++`). Per the transcription law the corpus consumes DECISION
//! CONTENT with ORIGINAL Rust fixtures below, never source verbatim. The
//! surface: a reusable explicit graph execution API over Apple Metal with
//! patchable replay groups -- an eligibility classifier refuses replay for
//! any dispatch carrying mutable surface (patch flags, scalars, undeclared
//! resources) with a TYPED fallback reason, a patch validator proves one
//! patch against the DECLARED resource contract (per-aspect patchability,
//! declared ranges, grid ceilings), and the evidence suite's claim-boundary
//! contract (in the sibling `rust_perf` rule) reports break-even honestly.
//!
//! Rules 1-2 (the faces ride their owners in earlier entries files; the
//! third rule and the wrap-custody face live in `rust_perf` / `sec`). Every
//! quote below was byte-verified against the clone at the pin this batch.

use crate::domains::kernel_opt::corpus::KernelRule;

pub fn entries() -> Vec<KernelRule> {
    vec![
        // ── Rule 1: the replay-eligibility classifier — a group that carries
        // ANY mutable surface must refuse replay with a TYPED reason, never a
        // silent silent slow path. ─────────────────────────────────────────
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "replay-group-eligibility-excludes-mutable-surface".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "MECHANISM (replay-group eligibility admission; category D — dispatch, the graph-exec REFUSE-AND-FALLBACK half): before a command group is planned into a reusable replay artifact (indirect command buffer / captured graph), every member dispatch must pass an ELIGIBILITY CLASSIFIER that refuses replay when the dispatch carries any MUTABLE SURFACE — runtime-patchable fields, scalar parameter updates, or resources whose access class is undeclared — and the refusal is TYPED: the classifier returns a reason enum, not a bool, and the plan builder records the reason so diagnostics can say WHY a group fell back. The source's classifier, verbatim (metal_backend.mm:496-512): 'static mg_icb_fallback_reason_t mg_icb_dispatch_eligible(const mg_exec_dispatch_t *dispatch)' — 'if (dispatch->patch_flags != 0 || dispatch->scalar_count != 0) { return MG_ICB_FALLBACK_PATCHABLE_FIELD; }' and per bound buffer 'if (!resource || resource->byte_count == 0 || resource->access == MG_RESOURCE_ACCESS_UNKNOWN) { return MG_ICB_FALLBACK_UNKNOWN_RESOURCE_ACCESS; }' else 'MG_ICB_FALLBACK_NONE'. The plan builder consumes it per node and on ANY non-NONE reason falls the whole group back to direct encode, recording the reason: 'exec->icb.groups_fallback = 1; exec->icb.last_fallback_reason = reason;' (metal_backend.mm:544-548) — multi-node groups fall back until eligibility proves out ('if (exec->node_count != 1) { exec->icb.groups_fallback = 1; exec->icb.last_fallback_reason = MG_ICB_FALLBACK_INELIGIBLE_NODE; return MG_STATUS_OK; }' :524-528), and the disabled/unsupported arms set their own reasons ('MG_ICB_FALLBACK_DISABLED' / 'MG_ICB_FALLBACK_UNSUPPORTED' :1178-1188). THE DECISION CONTENT: (a) admission is PER-DISPATCH and REFUSE-AND-FALLBACK — replay is the opt-in fast path, direct encode is always the correct answer, so an eligibility bug degrades to slower-but-right, never wrong-at-all (the mirror of the REWORK half: cuda-graph-scalar-via-device-pointer reworks a mutable scalar into device memory so a group BECOMES eligible; THIS refuses a group that carries un-reworkable mutable surface); (b) the reason is a VALUE the operator can read — the diagnostics block publishes planned/used/fallback counts plus the last typed reason ('out_diagnostics->icb_groups_planned = exec->icb.groups_planned;' patch.c:390, the icb_groups_* fields metal_graph.h:150), so a silent mass-fallback is observable, never a green zero; (c) the classifier's arms are CLOSED over the source's own declared hazards — patchable fields, scalars, undeclared access — a new hazard class means a new arm, never a widened silent pass. REGIME: any replay/capture fast path over heterogeneous work (captured CUDA graphs, ICB replay, recorded launch groups) where some members carry runtime-mutable parameters. ANTI-REGIME: uniform workloads where every member is trivially eligible (the classifier is dead weight — gate it behind a debug counter if so); fast paths that can CRASH on mutation (then the refusal is correctness, not just speed, and the same classifier applies). KINSHIP (do not double-count): graph-exec-update-gated-by-captured-topology-signature (B48) owns the UPDATE-legality axis (what may change on a captured exec AFTER capture, derived from the capture); declared-uncapturable-expert-declines-graphs owns the WHOLE-OP refusal face (an op that can never be captured declines the graph path entirely); gpu-resident-control-loop-static-icb-replay (B223, lithos) owns the GPU-resident CONTROL-FLOW axis (the per-token decision plane UNDER a static replay); graph-fallback-coverage-ladder owns the COVERAGE-COUNTING axis (the ladder's fallback COUNT is this rule's diagnostic feed — the typed-reason face landed there this batch); cuda-graph-scalar-via-device-pointer owns the REWORK half — compose: rework what can be reworked, refuse what cannot, count and type every refusal. PORTABLE (an admission-classifier law: typed refusal reasons over a fast-path population, ports to any replay/memo/bake design). NotBenchable here (replay-vs-direct-encode launch overhead is the foreign MG_ICB metric; no Metal validator in this corpus). Source: robbiemu/metal-graph (Apache-2.0) @ 0cd855b72d23c44aa0f7511aa6f01f694c00da39 — src/metal/metal_backend.mm:496-512 (the classifier), :514-548 (the plan builder's per-node consumption + the multi-node/unsupported arms), :1178-1188 (the disabled/unsupported reasons), src/core/patch.c:390 + include/metal_graph/metal_graph.h:150 (the diagnostics fields). Batch 341 / Plan 350 / Research 383.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// naive: plan every command group into the replay artifact and\n// replay blindly; a group carrying runtime-mutable surface either\n// crashes at patch time or silently re-encodes every launch with\n// nobody able to say why the fast path never engaged.\n\nenum Proto {} // no reason type: the classifier answers a bare bool\n\nfn plan_group(group: &[Dispatch], plan: &mut Plan) -> Result<(), Error> {\n    for d in group {\n        if d.patch_flags != 0 {\n            return Err(Error::PlanFailed); // one opaque error for every cause\n        }\n    }\n    plan.replay(group); // mutability later = corruption or silent fallback\n    Ok(())\n}\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// optimized: the eligibility classifier returns a TYPED reason; the\n// plan builder falls the whole group back to direct encode on any\n// non-eligible arm and RECORDS the reason + counts for diagnostics.\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Debug)]\nenum FallbackReason {\n    None,\n    IneligibleNode,\n    PatchableField,\n    UnknownResourceAccess,\n    Unsupported,\n    Disabled,\n    BackendError,\n}\n\nstruct Dispatch<'a> {\n    patch_flags: u32,\n    scalar_count: u32,\n    buffers: &'a [(u32, Access)], // (binding index, declared access)\n}\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\nenum Access {\n    ReadOnly,\n    Write,\n    Unknown,\n}\n\nfn dispatch_eligible(d: &Dispatch) -> FallbackReason {\n    if d.patch_flags != 0 || d.scalar_count != 0 {\n        return FallbackReason::PatchableField;\n    }\n    for &(_, access) in d.buffers {\n        if access == Access::Unknown {\n            return FallbackReason::UnknownResourceAccess;\n        }\n    }\n    FallbackReason::None\n}\n\n#[derive(Default)]\nstruct IcbDiagnostics {\n    groups_planned: u32,\n    groups_used: u32,\n    groups_fallback: u32,\n    last_fallback_reason: FallbackReason,\n}\n\nfn plan_group(group: &[Dispatch], diag: &mut IcbDiagnostics) {\n    diag.groups_planned += 1;\n    for d in group {\n        let reason = dispatch_eligible(d);\n        if reason != FallbackReason::None {\n            // the WHOLE group falls back to direct encode, reason recorded\n            diag.groups_fallback += 1;\n            diag.last_fallback_reason = reason;\n            return;\n        }\n    }\n    diag.groups_used += 1;\n    // ... plan into the replay artifact\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_classifier_refuses_with_typed_reasons() {\n    let ro = [(0u32, Access::ReadOnly)];\n    let clean = Dispatch { patch_flags: 0, scalar_count: 0, buffers: &ro };\n    assert_eq!(dispatch_eligible(&clean), FallbackReason::None);\n\n    let patched = Dispatch { patch_flags: 1, scalar_count: 0, buffers: &ro };\n    assert_eq!(\n        dispatch_eligible(&patched),\n        FallbackReason::PatchableField,\n        \"mutable surface refuses, reason typed\"\n    );\n\n    let unknown = [(1u32, Access::Unknown)];\n    let undeclared = Dispatch { patch_flags: 0, scalar_count: 0, buffers: &unknown };\n    assert_eq!(\n        dispatch_eligible(&undeclared),\n        FallbackReason::UnknownResourceAccess\n    );\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn one_bad_dispatch_falls_the_whole_group_back_and_is_counted() {\n    let ro = [(0u32, Access::ReadOnly)];\n    let bad = Dispatch { patch_flags: 2, scalar_count: 0, buffers: &ro };\n    let good = Dispatch { patch_flags: 0, scalar_count: 0, buffers: &ro };\n    let mut diag = IcbDiagnostics::default();\n    plan_group(&[good, bad, good], &mut diag);\n    assert_eq!(diag.groups_planned, 1);\n    assert_eq!(diag.groups_used, 0, \"no partial replay: the group is the unit\");\n    assert_eq!(diag.groups_fallback, 1);\n    assert_eq!(diag.last_fallback_reason, FallbackReason::PatchableField);\n}\n".to_string(),
        },
        // ── Rule 2: the patch validator — one patch is proven against the
        // DECLARED resource contract per aspect, never against the binding
        // table alone. ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "patch-validity-against-declared-resource-contract".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "MECHANISM (patch-time contract validation; category D — dispatch, host-orchestration): a binding answers WHICH resource sits at WHICH index; a RESOURCE CONTRACT answers what the replayed command is ALLOWED to do with it — declared byte range, declared access, per-aspect patchability flags, declared grid ceilings — and every runtime patch is VALIDATED AGAINST THE CONTRACT before it is applied, per aspect, with the failure named. The source's law, verbatim (patch.c): 'dispatch buffer patch requires a declared resource range' (:162) — a patchable binding with no declared nonzero range is a construction error, not a runtime surprise; 'dispatch grid exceeds declared maximum' (:113) — a grid patch is proven against the DECLARED ceiling; 'dispatch buffer patch range is incompatible' (:78) — the replacement (alignment + length) is proven against the declared range; 'field is not declared patchable' (:168) — the per-aspect flag gates each field class SEPARATELY (buffer / range / scalar), so declaring one aspect patchable never silently enables the others. Binding-vs-contract is the separation the whole design rests on: the binding table maps indices to resources at plan time; the contract (ranges, access, ceilings, patchability) is declared at construction and is what the patch is PROVEN against at update time — the two structures answer different questions and are validated by different code. DECISION CONTENT: (a) a patchable binding REQUIRES a declared nonzero range at construction (:162's refusal is at construction, before any patch exists) — patchability is promised only where the contract can prove a fit; (b) per-aspect patchability flags gate buffer/range/value changes SEPARATELY — one flag per aspect, never one global 'patchable' bit; (c) every patch's replacement is proven against the declared contract — alignment, range fit, ceiling fit — before apply, and the failure names the violated clause; (d) undeclared/unknown access refuses at the eligibility layer (the sibling rule replay-group-eligibility-excludes-mutable-surface owns that classifier — compose: eligibility gates the group at plan time, contract validation gates each patch at update time). REGIME: any replay artifact accepting runtime updates (CUDA-graph parameter patches, ICB replays with patched bindings, recorded-launch templates); a patch table designed without a declared contract (ranges unknown until a bad patch arrives). ANTI-REGIME: fully-static replays (no patch surface — the validator is dead code); patch tables whose contract IS the binding table (the two-questions collapse this rule forbids). KINSHIP (do not double-count): graph-exec-update-gated-by-captured-topology-signature (B48) owns UPDATE-LEGALITY from the CAPTURED SHAPE (what classes of change the capture even permits — topology-derived); THIS owns the per-PATCH proof against the DECLARED contract (range/alignment/ceiling fit — contract-derived); captured-graph-bakes-buffer-addresses owns the BAKED-ADDRESS hazard (addresses frozen into the capture); cuda-graph-scalar-via-device-pointer owns the device-side scalar REWORK; pool-slot-write-coalescing-owns-only-declared-ranges owns the write-range DISCIPLINE face (only declared ranges may be written — the same declared-range vocabulary on the pool axis). PORTABLE (a contract-validation law: separate what-is-bound from what-is-allowed; prove every update against the allowed). NotBenchable here (patch-validate overhead is host-orchestration latency below any corpus bar; the fixture pins the refusal arms). Source: robbiemu/metal-graph (Apache-2.0) @ 0cd855b72d23c44aa0f7511aa6f01f694c00da39 — src/core/patch.c:78, :113, :162, :168 (the four named refusals), the binding-vs-contract separation (the mg_dispatch_resource_desc_t declared access/range fields the validators read). Batch 341 / Plan 350 / Research 383.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// naive: the binding table IS the contract -- a patch names an\n// index and new bytes; validation asks only 'is the index known?',\n// never 'is the change within what the contract declared?'. Ranges\n// and ceilings are discovered at patch time by corruption.\n\nfn apply_patch(bindings: &[(u32, usize)], idx: u32, off: usize, bytes: &[u8]) {\n    if !bindings.iter().any(|(i, _)| *i == idx) {\n        panic!(\"unknown binding\"); // the ONLY check\n    }\n    // writes at `off` regardless of the declared range; grid patches\n    // unbounded; a non-patchable field patched anyway.\n    let _ = (off, bytes);\n}\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// optimized: the DECLARED contract is a first-class structure\n// (per-aspect patchability, ranges, ceilings) and every patch is\n// proven against it per aspect with the violated clause named.\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\nenum Aspect { Buffer, Range, Grid }\n\nstruct Contract {\n    patchable: [bool; 3], // one flag per aspect, never a global bit\n    range: Option<(usize, usize)>, // declared [offset, len), None = unpatchable\n    align: usize,\n    max_grid: u64,\n}\n\nenum PatchError {\n    NotDeclaredPatchable(Aspect),\n    NoDeclaredRange,\n    RangeIncompatible,\n    GridExceedsDeclaredMaximum,\n}\n\nfn validate_patch(\n    c: &Contract,\n    aspect: Aspect,\n    new_off: usize,\n    new_len: usize,\n    new_grid: u64,\n) -> Result<(), PatchError> {\n    let slot = match aspect {\n        Aspect::Buffer => 0,\n        Aspect::Range => 1,\n        Aspect::Grid => 2,\n    };\n    if !c.patchable[slot] {\n        return Err(PatchError::NotDeclaredPatchable(aspect));\n    }\n    if aspect == Aspect::Grid {\n        if new_grid > c.max_grid {\n            return Err(PatchError::GridExceedsDeclaredMaximum);\n        }\n        return Ok(());\n    }\n    let (base, len) = c.range.ok_or(PatchError::NoDeclaredRange)?;\n    if new_off % c.align != 0 || new_off + new_len > base + len {\n        return Err(PatchError::RangeIncompatible);\n    }\n    Ok(())\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_four_refusals_are_named_by_their_clause() {\n    let c = Contract {\n        patchable: [true, true, true],\n        range: Some((0, 256)),\n        align: 16,\n        max_grid: 1024,\n    };\n    // a patchable buffer aspect with NO declared range is a construction\n    // error -- proven here via the Range arm refusing\n    let no_range = Contract { range: None, ..c };\n    assert_eq!(\n        validate_patch(&no_range, Aspect::Range, 0, 8, 0),\n        Err(PatchError::NoDeclaredRange)\n    );\n    // grid beyond the declared ceiling\n    assert_eq!(\n        validate_patch(&c, Aspect::Grid, 0, 0, 2048),\n        Err(PatchError::GridExceedsDeclaredMaximum)\n    );\n    // alignment + range fit\n    assert_eq!(\n        validate_patch(&c, Aspect::Range, 8, 16, 0), // 8 % 16 != 0\n        Err(PatchError::RangeIncompatible)\n    );\n    assert_eq!(validate_patch(&c, Aspect::Range, 240, 32, 0), Err(PatchError::RangeIncompatible));\n    // the in-contract patch passes\n    assert_eq!(validate_patch(&c, Aspect::Range, 16, 32, 0), Ok(()));\n    assert_eq!(validate_patch(&c, Aspect::Grid, 0, 0, 1024), Ok(()));\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn patchability_is_per_aspect_never_global() {\n    let c = Contract {\n        patchable: [true, false, true], // range declared unpatchable\n        range: Some((0, 256)),\n        align: 16,\n        max_grid: 1024,\n    };\n    assert_eq!(\n        validate_patch(&c, Aspect::Range, 16, 8, 0),\n        Err(PatchError::NotDeclaredPatchable(Aspect::Range)),\n        \"declaring buffer+grid patchable never enables the range aspect\"\n    );\n    assert_eq!(validate_patch(&c, Aspect::Buffer, 0, 8, 0), Ok(()));\n}\n".to_string(),
        },
    ]
}
